<section id="editors" class="flex flex-col gap-4 scroll-mt-4 border-t-2 border-eist pt-8">
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-1">
    <p class="eyebrow text-eist">Only editors see this part</p>
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">For editors</h2>
  </div>
  <p>
    Editors keep the course right: the lessons themselves, which real recordings learners hear, and the AI replies learners have questioned.
    Everything below is on pages learners can't open. Every change to a lesson is recorded, so nothing you do there is final.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Setting up</h3>
  <p>If you haven't already:</p>
  <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
    <li>
      <strong>An editor account here.</strong> If you can read this section, you have one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Telegram, to answer learners' questions.</strong> Questions from <strong>Ask a native speaker</strong> reach speakers through Abairt's Telegram bot.
      Install Telegram on your phone, then ask Seosamh to switch on answering questions for you on Abairt and send you an invite link.
      Open the link on your phone and tap <strong>Start</strong>; that connects you, and the link stops working once used.
      Questions then arrive in that chat as recordings, and you answer with a voice message.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An Abairt account, to fix things at the source.</strong> The recordings, transcriptions and translations behind the clips and conversations live on abairt.com.
      Hiding a clip here only hides it in this course; correcting its transcription or translation on Abairt fixes it for every app that uses it.
      Ask Seosamh for an account if you don't have one.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Editing a lesson</h3>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
    <li><strong>Edit</strong> is on each lesson, and beside each lesson on the Lessons page.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Lesson details</strong> has the title and the word list: each word's Irish, English, gender, genitive, plural and pronunciation, plus an optional source line under the English (book and page, or speaker and place), which learners see beside the word.
      The quiz is built from the word list, so changes there reach learners' reviews.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Intro video</strong> takes a YouTube or Vimeo link, an embed code, or a link to an .mp4 file, with an optional transcript.</li>
    <li><strong>Rich content</strong> holds free text, videos, images and PDFs, in any order. <strong>Lesson content</strong> is the grammar from the book.</li>
    <li><strong>AI tutor settings</strong> tell the Practise chat what to focus on for this lesson. Leave it empty for the general default.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Published</strong> decides whether learners can see the lesson. Drafts show a Draft label and only editors see them.
      <strong>Mark as reviewed</strong> records that someone has checked a draft; unchecked drafts show <strong>Needs review</strong> on the Lessons page.
    </li>
    <li><strong>View as learner</strong> shows the lesson without the editing buttons.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>History</strong> lists every change to the lesson, newest first, and who made it.
      <strong>Undo</strong> puts back what a change replaced, and the undo is recorded too.
    </li>
    <li><strong>New lesson</strong> is at the top of the Lessons page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="course-tools" class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2 scroll-mt-4">Tools for designing the course</h3>
  <p>
    All of these are linked from <strong>Course tools</strong>, a button at the top of the Lessons page. They show you where the course is thin; they are prompts, not lists of faults.
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
    <li>
      <strong>Proposals</strong> lists suggestions for the course from the designer or a contributor. Type an idea into the box at the top and it is saved exactly in your words, marked as an idea. Under each one, <strong>Discuss</strong>, <strong>Not now</strong> and <strong>Dismiss</strong> record your decision and who made it; a dismissed or deferred one can be reopened. Each proposal also has a discussion: add a comment under it and it is kept with who wrote it and when. A proposal can also carry evidence: the source (book and page, Abairt entry and speaker, or a link), a quote copied exactly from it and a link to audio, shown under the proposal. Nothing in the list changes a lesson. From a connected chat app you can also say an idea aloud and it is saved the same way (the <code>capture_idea</code> tool), and ask what is waiting (<code>list_ideas</code>), ask for the next few to decide, best first (<code>review_queue</code>: your own ideas before the agent's, then higher priority, then the one waiting longest, each in two lines), open one with its evidence and discussion (<code>get_proposal</code>), attach a source to it (<code>add_proposal_evidence</code>), add a comment (<code>comment_on_proposal</code>), and ask where a word is taught, heard and practised (<code>where_is_taught</code>), list the Mayo forms pairs with the lessons where the other form still appears (<code>mayo_forms</code>), search the whole course, drafts included, for any Irish or English word (<code>search_course</code>, the same search as the site's search box), ask what the course or one lesson is missing (<code>course_gaps</code>, the same checks as the Vocabulary gaps page), and start a session by asking what changed since you were last in (<code>what_changed</code>): which proposals were decided and by whom, new comments from other people, new evidence, new proposals, and what is still waiting, longest first. It only looks; nothing changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Vocabulary gaps</strong> lists words with no genitive, no plural or no recording of their own, words taught in more than one lesson,
      everyday themes with few or no words, lessons with no recordings, lessons with words but no exercises or phrases, lessons with words but no grammar notes, lessons with words but no listening clips, lessons with words but no reading text, lessons whose words have no source note, lessons whose recordings have no named speaker, lessons not yet marked reviewed, and lessons with no words at all. Open a lesson from any line and fix it there.
      Not every word can take a genitive or plural, so use your judgement.
      Beside a noun missing a plural or genitive, <strong>Look up in Abairt</strong> shows sentences where Mayo speakers use a word that begins like it, with the speaker, whether the entry is confirmed and a play button.
      <strong>Accept</strong> fills the empty field with that word (it never overwrites, and shows in the lesson's History so you can undo it); <strong>Dismiss</strong> stops that word being suggested again.
      Nothing is worked out from grammar rules: the app only offers words people said, so listen and check each is really the plural or genitive. Most unconfirmed entries are speech-recogniser text and may be wrong. The themes it checks come from <code>config/course_themes.yml</code>; ask Seosamh to change them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where is it taught</strong> answers "where does the course teach this word?" Type part of an Irish or English word and it lists each matching word in a lesson's word list, with a link to the lesson, whether it has a recording, how many Abairt listening clips and review questions use it, and the lessons that use it in a phrase or an exercise. It only looks; nothing changes. The same answer is available from a connected chat app as <code>where_is_taught</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Words learners miss</strong> lists the words learners have answered wrongly most often in review over the last 90 days, hardest first: how many answers were wrong out of how many, how many learners missed it, and its lesson. Only words answered at least three times are shown, and only learners count (not admins). A word near the top may need a recording, a clearer English meaning, a note, or a place earlier or later in the course; that is your call. It only looks; nothing changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mayo forms</strong> is a list of pairs: a Connemara (or other) form and the Mayo form that should be used instead, each with where it comes from.
      Use <strong>Add a pair</strong> to add one. The list then shows which lessons still use the other form, with a link to each. Nothing is swapped automatically; you change the lesson yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Usage</strong> shows week by week how many learners are active, how many come back, how many answers they give and how accurate they are.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Learners</strong> is where new sign-ups are approved and where you can see how each learner is getting on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Flagged replies</strong> is described below.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Recordings for words</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Abairt recordings</strong>, on each lesson, lists the words that have no recording of their own and finds Mayo recordings of them on Abairt, a word at a time as you scroll.
    Each suggestion has been run through ABAIR's speech recogniser: <em>checked</em> means it heard the word, otherwise it shows what it heard instead.
    Listen before approving. Learners hear a recording as soon as it's approved, credited to the speaker, and approving one sets that word's other suggestions aside.
    <strong>Approve checked recordings</strong> approves the best checked one for every word at once; listen to a few first.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Listening clips</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Listening clips</strong>, on each lesson, shows the short phrases from Abairt that learners get once they've finished the lesson: up to two per word,
    3 to 12 words long, kept only when the recogniser heard the word and most of the phrase. They're found automatically.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing needs approving here, but please look through them. <strong>Hide from learners</strong> any clip with a wrong or misleading translation,
    audio that doesn't match the words, or anything that isn't suitable. You can also hide a clip from its own page, and <strong>Show to learners again</strong> undoes it.
    A hidden clip's whole conversation is no longer offered unless another clip leads to it.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Flagged conversation replies</h3>
  <p>
    When a learner flags an AI reply in a conversation, a <strong>flagged replies</strong> button appears at the top of the Lessons page (and it's in Course tools).
    Each flag shows what the learner said and the reply, and whether ABAIR or OpenAI wrote it.
    Check the Irish, then <strong>Dismiss</strong> it. If the reply really was wrong, note what was wrong and pass it on to Seosamh:
    replies can't be edited, but a pattern of mistakes tells us what to change in how the conversation is set up.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-semibold text-lg mt-2">Other things to know</h3>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
    <li>New sign-ups wait for approval before they can use the course. Seosamh approves them for now.</li>
    <li>Search finds draft lessons too, for editors.</li>
    <li>
      The course can also be edited through Claude, using a connector that can read and change lessons, words and grammar but can't delete anything.
      Changes made that way appear in each lesson's History like any other. Ask Seosamh if you'd like it set up.
    </li>
    <li>
      The course doesn't use AI to write its Irish, and that includes the Claude connector: use it for structure, English and tidying, not for writing Irish.
      Lesson Irish should come from the book, from speakers, or from you.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>
